<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Otto II, Duke of Bavaria</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1206">1206</a>–<a href="/years/1253">1253</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Otto the Illustrious · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wittelsbach">House of Wittelsbach</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Otto II, called the Illustrious, was a Wittelsbach Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> from 1231 to 1253 and, through his marriage to Agnes of the Palatinate, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/count-palatine">Count Palatine</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> from 1228. He supported the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a> emperors against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> and was excommunicated. On his death his sons Louis II and Henry XIII divided the duchy in 1255, the first of many Wittelsbach partitions.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/otto-ii-duke-of-bavaria.jpg" alt="Otto II, Duke of Bavaria" width="320" height="364" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Otto_II_Wittelsbach.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Otto was born on 7 April 1206 at Kelheim, the son of Louis I and Ludmilla of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, and belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wittelsbach">Wittelsbach dynasty</a>. In 1222, at the age of sixteen, he was married at Worms to Agnes of the Palatinate, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-v-count-palatine-of-the-rhine">Henry V, Count Palatine of the Rhine</a>, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-lion">Henry the Lion</a>, and of Agnes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a>. Through this marriage the Wittelsbach inherited the Palatinate, which they kept until 1918. The lion also became a heraldic symbol in the coats of arms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> and the Palatinate from this time.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Otto extended his power base in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> by acquiring rich lands for the Wittelsbach. He gained Bogen in 1240, and Andechs and Ortenburg in 1248. With the county of Bogen the family also took over the white and blue lozenge pattern that has been the flag of Bavaria and of the Palatinate ever since. A poet, Reinbot von Dürne, was active at his court.</p><p>After a dispute with Emperor Frederick II had been settled, Otto joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a> party in 1241. His daughter Elisabeth was married to Frederick's son Conrad IV, and she later bore the title Queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. Because of this alliance the pope excommunicated Otto.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Otto died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landshut">Landshut</a> on 29 November 1253 and, like his forefathers, was buried in the crypt of Scheyern Abbey. He had five surviving children by Agnes: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-ii-duke-of-bavaria">Louis II, Duke of Bavaria</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-xiii-duke-of-bavaria">Henry XIII, Duke of Bavaria</a>; Elisabeth; Sophie, who married Count Gerhard IV of Sulzbach and Hirschberg in 1258; and Agnes, who became a nun at Segenstal Abbey. A further daughter died young and her name is not known. Her betrothal to Conrad in 1235 is recorded in the Annales sancti Rudberti Salisburgenses.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Otto II of Bavaria” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Through Conrad's daughter Agnes it passed to the Welf Henry of Brunswick, and in 1214 Emperor Frederick II granted it to Louis I of Bavaria of the house of Wittelsbach, whose son <b>Otto II</b> married the Welf heiress.</blockquote><a href="/empire/electoral-palatinate">Electoral Palatinate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1255 Duke <b>Otto II</b>'s sons divided the Duchy of Bavaria, the first of many Wittelsbach partitions.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-upper-bavaria">Duchy of Upper Bavaria</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Kelheim, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landshut">Landshut</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.92,11.87&amp;year=1236" aria-label="Kelheim on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4824 456 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Otto II, Duke of Bavaria"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5274 726h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5274 726h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.92,11.87&amp;year=1236">What was here in 1236? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
